full, intervenes on D_full. The expectation includes worlds where D_full output different and observation might not be full? They calculate inaccurate branch leads empty. So no conditioning on Obs=full. The mention of Obs=full may be typo or means the node with input full. We can adopt Eq(4) no conditioning. But the user may expect explanation that observations by changing node. Need mention ambiguity in source: Section 6 sometimes writes conditioning on Obs=full; however Section 5's Equation (4) and the \"change intervention node\" principle suggest the observation is treated as an input to the decision function, not as an evidential condition that severs dependencies. We'll follow Eq(4). Good.\n\nNeed maybe explain do operator on logical variable: Pearl do originally causal, but source reuses it given G encoding all subjunctive dependencies. do(D=a) sets D to a, cuts incoming arrows to D, propagates along outgoing arrows according to structural equations / conditional distributions. It preserves dependencies downstream of D and breaks correlations due to common causes not downstream? In Newcomb, D -> Act and D -> Prediction -> BoxB. Thus do(D=onebox) sets Act=onebox, Prediction=onebox with high prob, BoxB full. In smoking lesion, D -> Act but not Cancer/Lesion. Thus do(D=smoke) changes Act only (plus maybe utility function node?), not lesion. Need detail smoking lesion formalization with utility uncertainty. Source has two possible models P_S/P_R and two decision functions. A-FDT node for actual preferences controls action if lesion? Wait lesion determines whether agent likes smoking and thus which utility function? They formalize lesion causes preferences: two utility functions U_S (smoking +1000) and U_R (refrain? Actually U_R differs values smoking at -$1000? lesion causes love smoking? Let's inspect: lesion causes those afflicted to love smoking and cancer. They say two utility functions U_S and U_R, U_R differs from U_S in that it values smoking at -$1. There